Distinguish between level term assurance, decreasing term assurance, and convertible term assurance. For each type, describe:
(a) the structure of the sum assured;
(b) the primary market application in Nigeria; and (c) the key advantage and limitation of the product.   Level Term Assurance (a) Structure: The sum assured is fixed at the level specified at inception and remains constant throughout the policy term. Death benefit is identical in year 1 and year 20.(b) Nigerian application: Income replacement protection for families with dependants; business partnership protection (covering a fixed liability under a partnership agreement); mortgage protection on interest-only facilities where the outstanding balance remains constant.(c) Advantage: Simple, transparent, and predictable — the beneficiary knows exactly what will be paid. Limitation: Inflation erodes the real purchasing power of a fixed benefit over long terms. A ₦10m sum assured in 2005 had far greater real value than ₦10m in 2025, given Nigeria's cumulative inflation. No provision for benefit indexation is standard in the Nigerian market. Decreasing Term Assurance (a) Structure: The sum assured reduces year by year over the policy term, following a defined schedule (typically mirroring the reducing balance of a repayment loan).(b) Nigerian application: Repayment mortgage protection (capital-and-interest loan where the outstanding balance reduces each month); credit life insurance mandated by NIIRA 2025 for qualifying loans (covering the reducing outstanding balance); reducing business loan security.(c) Advantage: C

