Distinguish between the Mudarabah model and the Wakalah model for Takaful operations. In each case, explain:
(a) how the Takaful operator earns its compensation;
(b) who owns the underwriting surplus of the Participants' Risk Fund; and (c) the principal advantage and disadvantage of the model from the perspectives of the operator and the participant. Which model is dominant in Nigeria and why?   Mudarabah Model (a) Operator compensation: The operator shares in the underwriting surplus of the PRF — typically 25–50% of the surplus after claims, reserves, and expenses. The operator also typically takes a share of investment returns from the PIF (e.g., 20–30% of PIF profits). Income is entirely variable and contingent on good underwriting and investment performance.(b) PRF surplus ownership: Surplus is shared between the operator (its Mudarabah share) and the participants (the balance). Neither party owns the entire surplus.(c) Advantages and disadvantages:• For the operator — advantage: high upside income in profitable years; disadvantage: zero income from the PRF in years of deficit (though the operator still earns the investment profit share).• For the participant — advantage: operator has a direct financial incentive to manage the PRF prudently (low claims, good investment returns); disadvantage: surplus return is reduced by the operator's share; uncertainty about what share of surplus they will receive. Wakalah Model (a) Operator compensation: A fixed Wakalah fee, agreed in advance as a percentage of contributions (typically 15–35%). The fee is earned regardless of underwr

