Compare and contrast co-insurance and Yearly Renewable Term (YRT) as life reinsurance methods, with specific reference to:
(a) what is ceded to the reinsurer in each case;
(b) the balance sheet and cash flow implications for the cedant insurer;
(c) which method is more appropriate for a pure term assurance product and why;
(d) which method is more appropriate for a 20-year participating endowment product and why; and (e) how the IFRS 17 accounting treatment of reinsurance assets differs between the two methods.   Part (a) — What is Ceded Co-insurance (proportional): The cedant cedes a defined percentage (e.g., 40%) of the policy in its entirety — premium income, policy reserve, and death benefit liability. The reinsurer effectively co-owns the policy. The reinsurer pays the cedant a ceding commission to compensate for the acquisition and administration costs the cedant bears.YRT: The cedant cedes only the Net Amount at Risk (NAR) — the mortality risk above the policy reserve — on an annual basis. No reserve is ceded. The cedant pays the reinsurer an annual risk premium based on the NAR × the treaty mortality rate. The YRT is renewed each year (hence 'yearly renewable') and the NAR and premium change annually as the reserve grows and the NAR reduces. Part (b) — Balance Sheet and Cash Flow Implications Co-insurance:• Balance sheet: The cedant's gross policy reserve is replaced by a lower net reserve (after the reinsured portion). The reinsurance asset (the ceded reserve) appears on the cedant's balance sheet.• Cash flow: The cedant receives a large upfront ceding commission w

