NIIRA 2025 has elevated the ERM requirements for Nigerian life insurers to a level broadly comparable with international best practice, including mandatory ORSA, RBC capital adequacy, and formal risk governance obligations. However, the effectiveness of these requirements depends on more than regulatory compliance — it depends on genuine risk culture within the insurer. Critically evaluate:
(a) the distinction between compliance-driven ERM (Phase 1) and culturally embedded ERM (Phase 4), and the governance and leadership conditions required for the transition;
(b) the role of the Board and the CRO in establishing a genuine risk culture, with reference to the common governance failures identified in this chapter;
(c) the adequacy of NAICOM’s supervisory toolkit under NIIRA 2025 to identify and address ERM culture failures, as distinct from technical compliance failures; and (d) the implications of weak ERM culture for the long-term financial stability of the Nigerian life insurance market.   Thesis NIIRA 2025's ERM requirements are technically well-designed and internationally benchmarked. However, regulations create compliance infrastructure — they cannot create risk culture. The history of corporate governance failures globally teaches that the most dangerous period in regulatory reform is when organisations appear compliant but have not yet developed genuine risk consciousness. Nigeria's life insurance market is entering precisely this phase. (a) Compliance-Driven (Phase 1) vs Culturally Embedded (Phase 4) ERM Phase 1 — Compliance-driven ERM: The organisation establishes
